<Base title="Partitioning and sharding" description="Choose partition and shard keys from queries and transaction boundaries, then follow a live tenant move through copying, verification, fencing and cutover.">
 <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p><h1>Partitioning and sharding</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational further reading</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/scaling/">Scaling a relational database ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>A large table can be divided into smaller pieces while remaining one logical collection of records. The boundary might follow dates, tenants or ranges of identifiers. That choice determines which pieces a query needs, which records move together, and what a database can enforce within each piece.</p>
  <p><strong>Table partitioning</strong> often means dividing a table inside one database system. PostgreSQL can route an October order to an October child table while the application queries their shared parent. <strong>Sharding</strong> distributes ownership of subsets across databases or nodes. A router might send tenant 7 to server A and tenant 8 to B. The first arrangement can improve local access and maintenance; the second can give independent subsets different write resources.</p>
  <p>The vocabulary overlaps between products, so follow the actual arrangement. Where are the records stored? Who accepts a write? Can one transaction or constraint cover the records involved? We will first use real PostgreSQL range partitions, then follow a tenant-based shard design and a move from one owner to another.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#local-partitions">Local partitions and pruning</a></li><li><a href="#keys">Keys and maintenance</a></li><li><a href="#shard-key">Choosing a shard key</a></li><li><a href="#cross-shard">Queries and rules across shards</a></li><li><a href="#moving-data">Moving live data</a></li><li><a href="#cutover">Verification and cutover</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="local-partitions"><h2>Local partitions and pruning</h2>
   <p>Suppose orders carry a reporting date, an order ID and a tenant ID. We want to manage old months separately and often query one recent month. In PostgreSQL 18, this declaration makes the date the partition key:</p>
   <Sql code={partitions} label="Define a parent table and two monthly partitions on one PostgreSQL server" />
   <p>The parent names the logical table. Its child partitions hold the rows. October accepts dates from 1 October inclusive to 1 November exclusive; November starts where October stops. An inserted row is routed by <code>placed_on</code>. All these tables still share the server’s compute, memory and ordinary transaction machinery.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=partition-pruning">partition-pruning lesson</a> creates the schema and three records. Order 1 belongs to tenant 7 on 15 October for €25. Order 2 belongs to tenant 8 on 20 October for €8. Order 3 belongs to tenant 7 on 1 November for €40. Its first result includes <code>tableoid::regclass</code>, which displays the child relation containing each row. Use the lesson’s setup instead of also running the declaration above against those same existing tables.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Orders placed into monthly partitions"><table><thead><tr><th>Child table</th><th>Order</th><th>Tenant</th><th>Date</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>October</td><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>15 October</td><td>€25</td></tr><tr><td>October</td><td>2</td><td>8</td><td>20 October</td><td>€8</td></tr><tr><td>November</td><td>3</td><td>7</td><td>1 November</td><td>€40</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>An October query cannot match anything in November’s range. <strong>Partition pruning</strong> uses that fact to exclude the irrelevant child. After running the lesson setup, inspect the actual read:</p>
   <Sql code={narrow} label="Inspect the partitions used for an October query" />
   <p>The checked plan reads <code>cap_orders_oct</code> and emits two rows; it does not scan <code>cap_orders_nov</code>. This is real pruning, but three rows do not establish a production speedup. Inside the selected partition the engine still needs an access method. It may scan the child or use a suitable index, depending on the query and data.</p>
   <p>Now ask for tenant 7 without mentioning a date. That tenant has one order in each child, and the partition bounds contain no reason to exclude either:</p>
   <Sql code={broad} label="Inspect a tenant query whose matches span both months" />
   <p>The plan includes both partitions, combined by an <code>Append</code> operation in the checked run. The date split has not become a tenant lookup index. A tenant index inside each child could help find its matches, while a query spanning all months may still visit many children. Choose partition boundaries from the common predicates and maintenance operations, then inspect the plans those queries actually receive.</p>
   <p>Pruning is not restricted to this literal example: an engine can sometimes eliminate partitions when parameters become known during execution. Its ability depends on the predicates, preparation mode and implementation. Test the application’s actual statement; a date-related expression that looks equivalent to a person is not evidence that its plan excludes the same children.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="keys"><h2>Keys and maintenance</h2>
   <p>The chosen partition key also affects other guarantees. Our primary key is the pair <code>(placed_on, order_id)</code>. That permits order ID 1 on 15 October and order ID 1 on 5 November because the complete pairs differ. PostgreSQL requires a partitioned table’s ordinary unique or primary-key constraint to include all partition-key columns, so separate child indexes can establish the declared uniqueness. It has not established that <code>order_id</code> alone is unique everywhere.</p>
   <p>If the application needs a globally unique order identity, choose how to preserve it. A different partition key may keep every occurrence of an ID in one partition. A separate authoritative identity table can enforce IDs before related partitioned facts are created, with a transaction maintaining their relationship. Generated identifiers can make accidental collisions unlikely, but generation and database enforcement answer different questions. Adding a date to a key just to satisfy a declaration changes the rule unless that pair is the intended identity.</p>
   <p>Missing future ranges affect availability. The lesson tries a 1 December insert before creating December’s partition. PostgreSQL rejects it because no child accepts the value. Creating ranges ahead of demand, monitoring their coverage, and deliberately deciding whether to allow a default partition belong to the operating plan. A default destination can retain unexpected values, but those rows still need inspection and may complicate attaching a later explicit range.</p>
   <p>Retention is another reason to choose dates. Detaching an old partition can remove a whole range from the parent’s query surface; deleting its detached storage is a separate decision. Check the retention policy, references, exports and recovery needs before treating detachment as deletion. Attaching or detaching also acquires locks, and validating an attached table can scan its contents. PostgreSQL has different lock requirements for different forms of these operations; the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html">partitioning documentation</a> is part of planning the live change.</p>
   <p>More pieces are not automatically better. Very many partitions add metadata, planning and maintenance work. A monthly boundary may suit month-based retention; splitting every hour could add hundreds of children without helping the important queries. Compare realistic range counts as well as row counts. Local partitioning earns its place when the pieces are useful units of access or management.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="shard-key"><h2>Choosing a shard key</h2>
   <p>Now suppose independent tenants need more write capacity than one server can provide. We can give each tenant an owner. The <strong>shard key</strong> is the value used to route its data and requests; the routing map says which current database owns that key or key range. A hash can assign keys to logical buckets, while a directory assigns those buckets to nodes. Keeping logical placement distinct from a machine name makes later movement possible.</p>
   <p>For a tenant-local checkout, include tenant ID on orders, lines and tenant-specific products. Place the same tenant’s related records together. This is <strong>co-location</strong>: their relationship is reflected in placement, so a join or transaction can remain on one owner. Route a request only after establishing its authorised tenant; an arbitrary ID supplied by a browser is not permission to query another tenant’s shard.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Tenant-based ownership and related records"><table><thead><tr><th>Owner</th><th>Records kept together</th><th>Local operation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>Tenant 7’s orders, lines, products and stock</td><td>Tenant 7’s checkout</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Tenant 8’s orders, lines, products and stock</td><td>Tenant 8’s checkout</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>If orders were routed by order ID but products by product ID, the same purchase could need two owners even though each table was evenly spread. The table relationship alone would not place its rows together. In <a href="https://docs.citusdata.com/en/stable/sharding/data_modeling.html">Citus’s tenant-distribution example</a>, the distribution column and co-location choices explicitly support this locality. Other distributed engines have their own placement and transaction rules.</p>
   <p>Balance must account for work as well as bytes. Ten thousand quiet tenants can be easier than one tenant whose checkout continuously updates a popular stock record. Giving the busy tenant a dedicated shard can isolate it from neighbours, but does not split its own load. Subdividing that tenant by warehouse or another operational boundary may help if the business can keep most operations within those pieces. It may also require coordination for transfers or purchases spanning warehouses.</p>
   <p>Range keys and hashes produce different distributions. A date range can concentrate all new writes in the newest range. Hashing tenant IDs can spread many tenants more evenly, but all work for one tenant still shares its placement. Hashing individual order IDs spreads that tenant’s orders at the cost of tenant-wide access. There is no key that distributes every workload while keeping every relationship local; test the expensive operations and the largest tenants before adopting one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cross-shard"><h2>Queries and rules across shards</h2>
   <p>A query with an authorised tenant ID can route directly. A report for every tenant may instead send work to all owners and combine their results. For a global sales total, each shard can calculate a subtotal and the coordinator can add those subtotals. For the newest twenty orders globally, each shard can supply candidates that the coordinator merges under a common ordering. A slow or unavailable shard now affects completion of the global answer.</p>
   <p>The result’s time meaning matters too. Independent reads on A and B do not automatically describe one common snapshot. A transfer recorded across owners could appear in one subtotal before its other side appears in the other. Use the distributed engine’s documented consistent-read facility when the report needs it, or define the report as an eventually reconciled view with the appropriate copying and freshness rules. Combining successful queries does not invent a shared transaction boundary.</p>
   <p>Constraints have the same ownership problem. Suppose catalogue code <code>BLUE-MUG</code> must be unique across all tenants. A local unique index on A and another on B can each accept that code once; neither sees the other insert. Options include putting that namespace under one owner, using a coordinated global uniqueness facility supplied by the engine, or changing the business rule to uniqueness within a tenant. A cross-shard preflight SELECT followed by independent inserts is still a race.</p>
   <p>Similarly, transferring value between two independently owned accounts needs a protocol that preserves the required outcome across failures. Some systems provide distributed transactions; others require a workflow with durable intermediate states and recovery. Do not split a previously local invariant merely because moving its tables is mechanically possible. Establish which component owns the decision and how a participant’s failure is resolved.</p>
   <p>These costs do not make sharding inherently unsuitable. They tell us why a tenant key often works well for a multi-tenant application whose important work is already tenant-local, while an application full of global transactions may need a different arrangement. Keep enough headroom and redundancy for each owner: splitting authoritative data means an unavailable owner affects its subset even when all other shards are healthy.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="moving-data"><h2>Moving live data</h2>
   <p>A routing decision eventually changes. A node fills up, a tenant becomes busy, or hardware is replaced. Moving tenant 7 from A to B must preserve its data and its write authority while the application continues operating. The difficult interval is between taking the first copy and deciding that B is ready.</p>
   <p>Start with two orders on A: order 1 has amount 25 at revision 1, and order 2 has amount 8 at revision 1. B copies that snapshot. While the copy is underway, the application corrects order 1 to 30, deletes order 2 and inserts order 3 for 12. The old and current sets now look like this:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="A copied tenant snapshot missing later changes"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>B’s initial copy</th><th>A after live changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Amount 25, revision 1</td><td>Amount 30, revision 2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Amount 8, revision 1</td><td>Deleted</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Absent</td><td>Amount 12, revision 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>Both sides contain two rows. A row-count comparison would approve a destination with a wrong amount, a stale deleted row and a missing new row. Copy completion alone does not establish that the copy includes the writes accepted during copying.</p>
   <p>A live mover needs a consistent initial snapshot and a change stream covering the interval from that snapshot onward, with no gap between them. The copying mechanism must coordinate those positions. Starting a log reader after a bulk export finishes would miss changes made during the export. Recording only inserts would miss the correction and deletion in our example.</p>
   <p>Keep A as the sole writer during bulk copying. Replay the subsequent changes onto B in the required order, retaining enough durable progress to resume if the mover stops. Repeated delivery needs a replay rule that does not apply an increment twice or let an older version overwrite a newer one. Deletes need an explicit representation in that stream; a row’s absence from a later scan is not a safe substitute for tracking its deletion.</p>
   <p>The mechanism should move the co-located group together. Moving tenant 7’s orders while leaving its lines behind breaks the locality we chose. Citus’s <a href="https://docs.citusdata.com/en/stable/admin_guide/cluster_management.html#shard-rebalancing">shard-rebalancing documentation</a> describes an implementation that moves co-located shards, uses logical replication during copying, and has a short write-lock interval for the final ownership change. A custom application migration needs equivalent answers; issuing writes independently to both copies is not a general solution to failures between those writes.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=shard-cutover">shard-cutover lesson</a> keeps source, destination, changes and an ownership record in one database. It deliberately lets you inspect the stale copy, then applies the three changes. This is a model of the invariants to preserve, not an implementation of change-data capture, a network protocol or a production migration tool.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cutover"><h2>Verification and cutover</h2>
   <p>Replaying quickly is not enough to establish the final boundary while writes continue arriving. One workable cutover protocol briefly stops admitting new tenant writes on A, drains or resolves every already-admitted writer, and records a final change-stream position. B must apply through that position. Requests arriving during the pause need a defined queue, retry or temporary-failure response; “online migration” does not promise that every write has zero additional delay.</p>
   <p>Verification must compare equivalent states. Checks performed while A continues changing and B remains behind can disagree for a legitimate timing reason. At the final drained boundary, compare the identities and required values, and verify that the destination has the constraints, indexes, schema, permissions and relevant identity-generation state it will need. Counts and range-level checksums can help locate differences in a large move, but they are not a substitute for a defined reconciliation procedure.</p>
   <p>In the small lesson, both copies are ordinary tables with the same four columns: tenant ID, order ID, amount and revision. We can compare the full rows in both directions. <code>EXCEPT</code> returns rows present on its left but absent on its right. Once writes are paused and the final three changes have been applied, this query must report zero:</p>
   <Sql code={comparison} label="Compare the complete toy source and destination at a common stopped boundary" />
   <p>If it does not, keep the new write path closed and repair the copy. The lesson’s ownership update is conditional on this same check, so bypassing the comparison in the editor does not silently approve mismatching rows. For a large distributed move, verification and the ownership decision need the mover’s coordinated protocol; this one-database query cannot supply atomicity across independent servers.</p>
   <p>Only then switch the authoritative owner to B and reopen writes. Give the new ownership a generation, such as 2, so a delayed request carrying A’s generation 1 can be recognised. Recognition is useful only when it is enforced. All admitted writes must be subject to the current ownership decision, including work arriving over old connections or stale routing caches. A forgotten writer that can still write directly to A defeats the scheme.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Write ownership through a tenant move"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>A may accept tenant writes</th><th>B may accept tenant writes</th><th>Required evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snapshot and replay</td><td>Yes, generation 1</td><td>No</td><td>Gap-free copying and resumable progress</td></tr><tr><td>Final boundary</td><td>No new admissions; drain earlier work</td><td>No</td><td>Final source position, applied changes and matching state</td></tr><tr><td>After cutover</td><td>No; stale authority rejected</td><td>Yes, generation 2</td><td>Exclusive authority and current routing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>The lesson tries an old A/generation-1 write after the switch and gets no updated row. A handler must treat that as a rejected or stale route, not success, then resolve or retry its operation safely against the current owner. The model checks ownership sequentially on one connection. With concurrent writers, the ownership check and admitted write must be protected against cutover happening between them, for example by a coordinated locking or fencing protocol. A real distributed service needs that enforcement at its write boundary; a copied generation number in an application cache is insufficient.</p>
   <p>Keep the source copy until the recovery plan permits its removal, but remember what changes after cutover. The lesson writes amount 35 to B while A still holds 30. Switching routing back to A now loses that accepted change. Reversal requires moving or reconciling B’s new history while preserving one writer, not just restoring yesterday’s route.</p>
   <p>Test interruption at each phase: the bulk copy stops, replay restarts, validation finds a missing deletion, the controller fails during the pause, or a client’s commit reply disappears near cutover. Persist enough migration state to discover which phase is authoritative after restart. The useful completion condition is that the destination has the required history, new writes have one owner, stale writers are fenced, and the application can recover interrupted operations. Merely finishing a transfer is only one step towards that result.</p>
  </section>
